update public.study_materials set content = '<h2>Ethics, Security &amp; Impact</h2>
<p><strong>Cambridge IGCSE 0478 (2026–2028):</strong> ethics &amp; professional codes, security threats, malware, online safety, environmental/employment impact.</p>

<h3>1. Ethics and codes</h3>
<p>ACM/IEEE-CS/BCS codes: public interest, competence, integrity, privacy. Applying: identify the principle → apply to scenario → stakeholders affected → consequences.</p>

<h3>2. Threats</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Malware</strong>: virus (attaches, replicates via host), worm (self-spreads), trojan (disguised), spyware, ransomware.</li>
<li><strong>Social</strong>: phishing (fake emails/sites), pharming (DNS/hosts redirect), shoulder surfing, brute force.</li>
<li><strong>Technical</strong>: interception (packet sniffing), SQL injection (malicious input into queries), DoS (flooding services).</li>
</ul>

<h3>3. Cyber security</h3>
<p>Cyber security protects data, systems and users from attack. A <strong>vulnerability</strong> is a weakness an attack can use; a <strong>threat</strong> is what exploits it.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Vulnerabilities</strong>: default or weak passwords, unpatched software, misconfigured access rights, unsecured or public Wi-Fi, removable media (memory sticks), insider threat, unencrypted data in transit.</li>
<li><strong>Malware</strong>: virus (needs a host file and replicates when that file runs), worm (self-spreads across a network), trojan (disguised as useful software), spyware (records activity), ransomware (encrypts files and demands payment), adware.</li>
<li><strong>Social engineering</strong>: phishing (fake email or site asks for details), pharming (malicious redirect via DNS or the hosts file), blagging (invented story), shoulder surfing, brute-force and dictionary attacks.</li>
<li><strong>Technical attacks</strong>: interception / packet sniffing on an unsecured network, SQL injection (malicious input typed into a query field), denial of service and DDoS (flooding a service so it cannot respond), session hijacking.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Protection</strong>: strong unique passwords plus 2FA; biometric authentication (fingerprint, face); encryption so intercepted data is unreadable (plaintext is turned into ciphertext with a key — HTTPS/TLS does this on websites); firewalls filtering traffic by IP address and port; anti-malware that scans, blocks and quarantines; patching and updates; user access levels so staff reach only what they need; regular backups; user training; penetration testing to find holes first.</p>
<p><strong>Answer shape:</strong> name the threat precisely (pharming is not phishing), then give the <em>matched</em> defence — pharming → keep DNS servers and hosts files secure and check HTTPS certificates.</p>

<h3>4. Digital currency</h3>
<p>A <strong>digital currency</strong> exists only in electronic form. A <strong>cryptocurrency</strong> (for example Bitcoin) is a decentralised digital currency secured by cryptography and recorded on a <strong>blockchain</strong>.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Blockchain</strong>: a distributed ledger — every node holds a copy. Transactions are grouped into blocks, and each block stores the hash of the previous block, so altering an old record breaks the chain and is rejected by the network.</li>
<li><strong>Keys and wallets</strong>: the owner holds a public key (the address others pay to) and a private key (proves ownership and signs a payment). A wallet stores these keys — lose the private key and the money is gone.</li>
<li><strong>Transactions</strong>: broadcast to the network, checked by nodes and added to a block; the verifier (miner) is rewarded. No bank or central authority is involved.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Advantages:</strong> quick international transfers, low transaction fees, no central authority to fail or censor, a transparent ledger, and coins cannot be counterfeited. <strong>Disadvantages:</strong> the value is very volatile, payments are irreversible, mining uses large amounts of energy, it appeals to criminals and ransomware demands, it needs internet access and technical care, and there is little consumer protection if a wallet is lost or stolen.</p>

<h3>5. Defences</h3>
<p>Antivirus/antispyware (scan, quarantine), firewalls (port/IP filtering), 2FA + biometrics, strong passwords + managers, encryption (HTTPS, VPN), access levels, patches/updates, user training.</p>

<h3>6. Online behaviour</h3>
<p>Digital footprint, cyberbullying, fake news, privacy settings, verification of sources.</p>

<h3>7. Wider impact</h3>
<p>Employment: automation displaces roles but creates IT jobs. Environment: e-waste, energy use vs paperless efficiency. Monitoring: workplace tracking, CCTV — balance with privacy laws (GDPR-style).</p>

<p><strong>Exam tips:</strong> name the threat precisely (pharming ≠ phishing), then the <em>matched</em> defence; ethics answers need principle + application, not opinion.</p>', updated_at = now() where id = '1ad7eb98-7831-4f23-85f8-9790a66e4ca4';
update public.study_materials set content = '<h2>Logic Gates &amp; Boolean Logic</h2>
<p><strong>Cambridge IGCSE 0478 (2026–2028):</strong> six gates, truth tables, building/describing circuits, problem-to-logic mapping.</p>

<h3>1. The six gates</h3>
<table border="1" cellpadding="6"><tr><th>A</th><th>B</th><th>AND</th><th>OR</th><th>NAND</th><th>NOR</th><th>XOR</th></tr>
<tr><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>1</td><td>1</td><td>0</td></tr>
<tr><td>0</td><td>1</td><td>0</td><td>1</td><td>1</td><td>0</td><td>1</td></tr>
<tr><td>1</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>1</td><td>1</td><td>0</td><td>1</td></tr>
<tr><td>1</td><td>1</td><td>1</td><td>1</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>0</td></tr></table>
<p>NOT inverts. NAND = AND + invert; NOR = OR + invert; XOR = 1 when inputs differ.</p>

<h3>2. Truth tables</h3>
<p>2 inputs → 4 rows; 3 inputs → 8 rows. Work gate by gate, adding intermediate columns for sub-circuit outputs.</p>

<h3>3. Logic expressions</h3>
<p>A Boolean expression describes a circuit in logic: X = A AND NOT B, or X = (A OR B) AND C. Brackets fix the order — work out bracketed parts first, then AND, then OR.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Circuit → expression</strong>: label the output of every gate (P, Q, …), write one expression per gate in order, then combine them at the final gate.</li>
<li><strong>Truth table → expression</strong>: mark the rows where the output is 1; for each such row write a term that is true only in that row (write NOT for an input that is 0, AND between the inputs); join the terms with OR. For XOR: X = (NOT A AND B) OR (A AND NOT B).</li>
<li><strong>Expression → truth table</strong>: add an intermediate column for each sub-expression before the output column.</li>
<li><strong>Rules used to simplify</strong>: A AND 1 = A; A AND 0 = 0; A OR 1 = 1; A OR 0 = A; A AND A = A; A AND NOT A = 0; A OR NOT A = 1; NOT(NOT A) = A; X OR (X AND Y) = X. (AS extension, 9618: De Morgan''s laws — NOT(A AND B) = NOT A OR NOT B and NOT(A OR B) = NOT A AND NOT B.)</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Example:</strong> a light X is on only when switch A is on and switch B is off → X = A AND NOT B, so X = 1 in exactly one row of its truth table (A = 1, B = 0).</p>

<h3>4. Problem → logic</h3>
<p>Define inputs/outputs first. Alarm rings (X=1) if sensor P triggered AND (door D OR window W): X = P AND (D OR W). Then draw the circuit: OR first, then AND.</p>

<h3>5. Describing circuits</h3>
<p>Write the expression from a diagram gate by gate; annotate output of each gate (X, Y) before combining.</p>

<p><strong>Exam tips:</strong> check your truth table row count before writing; in problem questions, state "input P = 1 means sensor triggered" — definitions earn marks.</p>', updated_at = now() where id = '410f9140-4fa3-467e-8cac-852bafc28653';
